{"prompt":"Implement the Rust translation of the C "ulidgen" project in the workspace root (plan.md, tasks.json, notes.md already exist). Use std-only, zero dependencies. Create:

1. Cargo.toml — package name "ulidgen", edition 2021, no dependencies.
2. src/lib.rs — a `pub fn ulidgen_r(ulid: &mut [u8; 27])` (or equivalent API) that mirrors this C code exactly in behavior:

```c
void
ulidgen_r(char ulid[27])
{
	static const char *b32alphabet = "0123456789ABCDEFGHJKMNPQRSTVWXYZ";
	char *buf = ulid + 10;
	int same = 1;
	ulid[26] = 0;
	struct timespec tv;
	clock_gettime(CLOCK_REALTIME, &tv);
	uint64_t t = tv.tv_sec*1000 + tv.tv_nsec/1000000;
	for (int i = 9; i >= 0; i--, t /= 32)
		if (ulid[i] != b32alphabet[t % 32])
			ulid[i] = b32alphabet[t % 32], same = 0;
	if (same) {             /* increment random part in place */
		int i = 15;
		while (i >= 0 && buf[i] == 'Z')
			buf[i--] = '0';
		if (i < 0) {
			/* restart 1ms + a bit later */
			nanosleep(&(struct timespec) { 0, 1234567 }, 0);
			ulidgen_r(ulid);
			return;
		}
		char *s = strchr(b32alphabet, buf[i]);
		if (s) {
			buf[i] = *(s+1);
			return;
		}
		/* else randomize again when we found invalid chars */
	}
	unsigned char rnd[16];
	if (getentropy(rnd, sizeof rnd) < 0)
		abort();
	for (int i = 0; i < 16; i++)
		buf[i] = b32alphabet[rnd[i] % 32];
}
```

Key semantics to preserve:
- The function is REUSABLE: it reads the previous ULID from the buffer (the caller keeps the same buffer across calls). If the new timestamp encodes to the same first 10 chars as the previous call, it increments the 16-char random part in place (Crockford base32 carry, 'Z' wraps to '0'); on full overflow it sleeps ~1.23ms and retries.
- Timestamp: current time in milliseconds since epoch, encoded as 10 Crockford base32 chars (t divided by 32 ten times, most significant first).
- Randomness: 16 random bytes, each mapped via byte % 32 into the alphabet. Use std-only: read 16 bytes from /dev/urandom (std::fs::File::open("/dev/urandom") + read_exact); on error, panic/abort like the C code.
- Output buffer: 27 bytes, last byte NUL in C; in Rust you can store 26 chars + NUL or just 26 chars — pick a clean API (e.g. `pub fn ulidgen_r(ulid: &mut [u8; 27])` keeping the NUL at index 26 for fidelity, or `pub fn ulidgen_r(prev: &mut Option<String>) -> String`). Document your choice.
- Thread safety: the C code is single-threaded; a simple approach is fine, but note tests may run in parallel threads — make the function take the previous ULID as a parameter (stateless) so it's thread-safe.

3. src/main.rs — CLI mirroring src/ulidgen.c:
```c
// usage: ulidgen [-n N | -t]
// -n N: print N consecutive ULIDs (default 1)
// -t: read lines from stdin, prefix each line with a ULID + space
// exits 0 on success, >0 on error
```
Use std::env::args manual parsing (support -n N and -t), line-buffered stdout for -t mode (flush after each line), exit code 0/1.

4. tests/test.rs — integration tests mirroring tests/test.c:
- test_ulid_length: generated ULID is 26 chars.
- test_ulid_structure: all chars in "0123456789ABCDEFGHJKMNPQRSTVWXYZ" (note: the C test had this commented out in main, but include it — it should pass).
- test_ulid_uniqueness: two consecutive ULIDs (same buffer/thread) differ.
- test_ulid_sortability: generate one, sleep 1.5ms, generate next, assert first < second lexicographically.

5. Run `cargo build` and `cargo test` in the workspace. Fix any errors until both pass.

6. Update tasks.json (mark done items) and append findings to notes.md (API choice, any deviations from C semantics, build/test results).

Report back: exact API you chose, build result, test results (which tests passed), and any deviations."}

